        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every mower sold today claims to mulch. Most of them are really side-discharge or bagging mowers that mulch when you fit a plug, and their decks are shaped to throw clippings out rather than hold them in. So our first filter was deck design: we favored mowers whose decks are built around mulching, such as Toro's Recycler, or whose blade systems are, such as EGO's Select Cut.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our second filter was power. Mulching loads a mower more heavily than discharging, because clippings stay under the deck and the blade cuts them several times. A mower with too little torque slows down, and a slow blade tears and smears rather than cuts, leaving wet green clumps. We looked at published torque ratings and at how each mower holds blade speed in thick grass.
