        <div id="deck-design" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Makes a Mower Mulch Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            A mulching mower does one thing differently from a discharging mower: it keeps clippings under the deck long enough to cut them again. A discharge mower cuts the grass once and throws it out of the chute. A mulching mower closes that chute and uses the shape of the deck and the airflow from the blade to circulate clippings back into the blade path, so a single blade of grass is cut into several small pieces before it falls. Small pieces drop down between the grass blades to the soil. Large pieces sit on top of the lawn and look untidy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck shape is the main difference. A dedicated mulching deck, such as Toro's Recycler, has a domed or tunnel-shaped underside and no permanent discharge opening, so the airflow circulates clippings instead of pushing them out. A convertible 3-in-1 deck has a discharge opening that is blocked with a plug when you want to mulch. That works, but the deck is shaped to throw clippings sideways or backward, so the circulation is less effective and the pieces are larger.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blade count and design matter too. Twin-blade systems, such as Toro's Atomic blades on the TimeMaster or EGO's stacked Select Cut blades, cut clippings twice on each pass and produce finer mulch. A single mulching blade with extra cutting surfaces along its curve does a similar job on a smaller scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck material affects mulching over time. Wet clippings stick to the underside of a deck, and the build-up disrupts the airflow that makes mulching work. Heavy steel and cast aluminum decks resist denting that traps debris, and a smooth underside is easier to scrape clean. A washout port, which lets you hose out the deck, is worth having on any mulching mower.
          </p>

