        <div id="blades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Mulching Blades vs Standard Blades
          </h2>

          <p>
            A standard, or high-lift, mower blade has a large upswept wing at its trailing edge. The wing generates strong lift, which stands the grass up for cutting and blows clippings out of the chute or up into the bag. That is exactly what you want for bagging, and less than ideal for mulching, because it throws clippings out of the blade path before they can be cut again.
          </p>

          <p>
            A mulching blade, sometimes called a 3-in-1 or all-purpose blade, has a longer, curved cutting edge and often additional cutting surfaces along the wing. It produces less lift and more circulation, so clippings stay in the deck and get cut several times. Many mowers ship with a general-purpose blade that compromises between the two, and fitting a proper mulching blade is one of the cheapest upgrades you can make to a mulching mower. Our <a href="/best-lawn-mower-blades/">best lawn mower blades</a> guide covers which replacement blades fit which mowers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sharpness matters even more for mulching than for bagging. A sharp blade slices each clipping cleanly, producing small pieces that fall to the soil. A dull blade tears grass, leaving shredded tips that brown and lose moisture, and it produces ragged, stringy clippings that clump together and sit on top of the lawn. If your mower mulched well last spring and leaves clumps now, the blade is the first suspect.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHARPENING RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sharpen a mulching blade every 20 to 25 hours of mowing.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">For a typical suburban lawn that works out to two or three times a season. Sandy soil, lots of leaves or hitting roots and stones wears the edge faster. A torn, brown-tipped lawn a day after mowing is the sign you have left it too long.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            How often to sharpen depends on how much you mow and what your mower hits. Our guide to <a href="/how-often-to-sharpen-mower-blades/">how often to sharpen mower blades</a> covers the signs and the method in detail. Keep a spare blade so you can swap it in and sharpen the dull one at your convenience.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution: always replace a blade with one specified for your mower. Blade length, center hole shape and lift all matter, and the wrong blade can reduce cutting quality, overload the engine or motor, or come loose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="one-third" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The One-Third Rule
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most important habit for mulching is never to remove more than one-third of the grass blade in a single mowing. If you keep your lawn at 3 inches, mow it when it reaches about 4.5 inches. Let it grow to 6 inches and you are asking the mower to mulch twice the volume of clippings, which no mower does well.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rule exists for the grass as much as for the mower. Cutting off more than a third of the leaf at once removes much of the plant's ability to photosynthesize and shocks it. The grass responds by putting energy into regrowing leaves rather than roots, which leaves the lawn weaker, more drought-sensitive and more open to weeds. A lawn that is regularly scalped this way thins out over a few seasons.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">ONE-THIRD RULE IN PRACTICE</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4.5 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">mow at this height to keep a 3-inch lawn</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">maximum share of the blade to remove per cut</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5-7 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">typical interval in peak growing season</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">good summer height for most cool-season grass</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For mulching, the rule is also what makes the clippings disappear. Short clippings, an inch or so at most, fall down between the grass blades and are out of sight by the next day. Long clippings from overgrown grass lie on top of the lawn, block light and look messy, however good the mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            In spring, when growth is fastest, that often means mowing every five days or so. If you fall behind, do not try to catch up in one pass. Raise the deck to its highest setting, mow, and then lower it over the next two or three mowings. Alternatively, bag or side discharge for one mowing and return to mulching once the lawn is back under control.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wet-grass" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Wet Grass and Clumping
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wet grass is the other main cause of poor mulching. Wet clippings are heavy and sticky, so instead of circulating in the deck they cling to its underside and to each other. The deck fills up, airflow drops, blade speed falls, and the mower leaves wet, green clumps behind it. Those clumps can smother the grass underneath and leave yellow patches if they are not broken up.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best fix is to wait. Mow mid-morning once the dew has dried, or late afternoon, and avoid mowing within a few hours of rain. If you have to mow wet grass, raise the cutting height a notch so the mower removes less, walk more slowly so the blade has time to process each clump, and consider switching to bagging or side discharge for that one mowing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery mowers are more sensitive to wet grass than gas ones, because the extra load drains the battery faster and some models reduce blade speed to conserve charge. If your battery mower has a boost or high-torque mode, use it in damp grass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the deck regularly. A layer of dried clippings under the deck reduces the space the airflow needs, and every mowing after that mulches a little worse. After mowing damp grass, disconnect the spark plug or remove the battery, tip the mower as the manual directs, and scrape the underside. A washout port makes this quicker on the mowers that have one.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you do leave clumps, they are easy to fix. Go over them again once they have dried, or rake them out. Do not leave them sitting on the lawn for more than a day or two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="leaves" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Mulching Leaves Instead of Raking
          </h2>

          <p>
            A good mulching mower can save you most of your autumn raking. Research from Michigan State University, among others, has found that shredding leaves into a lawn with a mulching mower does no harm to the grass and can improve the soil, as the finely chopped leaves break down and feed soil life. It also saves the time and the bags that raking and hauling would take.
          </p>

          <p>
            The key is how much you mulch at once. A thin layer of leaves, where you can still see grass between them, mulches easily. A thick layer does not: the mower cannot chop it finely enough, and the resulting mat smothers the grass over winter. So mulch leaves frequently through the autumn, weekly when trees are dropping heavily, rather than waiting for all the leaves to fall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aim for pieces around the size of a dime. At that size, they drop between grass blades and break down quickly. If your first pass leaves larger pieces, go over the area again, ideally at right angles to the first pass. Dry leaves shred much more easily than wet ones, so pick a dry afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            If there are too many leaves to mulch, bag them instead and use them as compost or garden mulch. EGO's Select Cut mowers include a high-lift bagging blade that works well for this, and any of the 3-in-1 mowers here can bag shredded leaves. For clearing leaves from beds and paths first, a good leaf blower saves a lot of time; see our <a href="/best-leaf-blower/">best leaf blower</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            One caution: leaves hide sticks, stones and nuts. Walk the lawn before mowing to remove anything that could damage the blade or be thrown.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Can a Battery Mower Mulch?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, but choose carefully. Mulching loads the blade motor more than discharging, because clippings stay under the deck and are cut several times. A battery mower with limited torque slows down under that load, and at lower blade speed it tears and smears grass rather than cutting it cleanly. Many cheaper battery mowers also reduce blade speed automatically to extend runtime, which saves charge at the cost of mulching quality.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best battery mowers have largely solved this. The EGO LM2156SP and LM2206SP are rated at 8.3 and 11.1 ft-lb of torque, and both use EGO's stacked Select Cut blades, which cut each clipping twice. The Toro 60V Recycler uses the same mulching deck as Toro's gas mowers. In weekly mowing of a lawn kept at a sensible height, all three mulch about as well as a good gas mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is runtime. Manufacturer runtime ratings assume dry, short grass. Mulching thick spring growth or damp grass uses energy much faster, and you should expect noticeably less than the rating. If your lawn is at the upper end of a battery mower's rated coverage, a second battery is a sensible purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a real advantage. A battery mower delivers full torque instantly and does not need to rev up, and most have a boost or high-torque mode for thick patches. Combined with a sharp blade and the one-third rule, a good battery mower is an excellent mulcher. See our <a href="/best-electric-lawn-mower/">best electric lawn mower</a> guide for more options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mulch-vs-bag" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When to Bag Instead
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mulching should be your default. Grass clippings are mostly water and break down quickly, returning nitrogen and other nutrients to the soil. University extension services commonly estimate that leaving clippings on the lawn can supply up to a quarter of its yearly nitrogen needs, which means you can use less fertilizer. Our <a href="/best-fertilizer-for-grass/">best fertilizer for grass</a> guide explains how to adjust your feeding if you mulch.
          </p>

          <p>
            A common myth is that mulched clippings cause thatch. They do not. Thatch is built up mainly from roots, stems and runners, which break down slowly, not from leaf clippings, which decompose within days. Mulching does not cause a thatch problem, though overfeeding and overwatering can.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are times to bag. If the lawn has become overgrown and you cannot follow the one-third rule, bag for one or two mowings. If there is a fungal disease in the lawn, bagging removes infected material instead of spreading it. If weeds have gone to seed, bagging avoids spreading their seeds across the lawn. And when there are too many leaves to mulch, bag them for compost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Slopes are another reason to mulch. A full bag adds weight behind the rear wheels, which makes a mower harder to control on a grade, so mulching is also the better choice on a sloped lawn. Our <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-hills/">best lawn mower for hills</a> guide covers which mowers handle slopes best.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 3-in-1 mower gives you all three options, and every mower in this guide can mulch, bag or side discharge. Use mulching most of the time, and switch only when one of these exceptions applies.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Grass Get Too Long</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                No mower mulches overgrown grass well. Removing more than a third of the blade leaves long clippings that sit on top of the lawn and clump. Mow often enough to follow the one-third rule, and if you fall behind, raise the deck and bring the lawn down gradually over a few mowings.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing Wet Grass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Wet clippings stick to each other and to the deck, reducing airflow and blade speed. The result is wet clumps that can smother the grass. Wait until the dew or rain has dried, and if you must mow wet grass, raise the deck, slow down and consider bagging for that mowing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Dull Blade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dull blade tears grass rather than cutting it, producing ragged clippings that clump and leaving brown, frayed tips on the lawn. Mulching is more sensitive to sharpness than bagging. Sharpen every 20 to 25 hours of mowing, and keep a spare blade so you are never tempted to put it off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Cleaning the Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dried clippings build up under the deck and reduce the space the airflow needs to circulate clippings. Each mowing then mulches a little worse. Disconnect the spark plug or battery and scrape the underside regularly, especially after damp mowings, or use the washout port if your mower has one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting to Mulch All the Leaves at Once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A mulching mower handles a thin layer of leaves easily but chokes on a thick one, leaving a mat that smothers the grass over winter. Mulch leaves weekly through the autumn while you can still see grass between them, and aim for pieces around the size of a dime.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mulching mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Toro 22-inch Recycler Max with Personal Pace is our top pick. Its deck is designed around mulching, with a domed shape and Vortex airflow that hold clippings under the blade for several cuts, and the 13-gauge steel deck is built to last. For battery power, the EGO LM2156SP with its stacked Select Cut blades is the best choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is mulching better than bagging grass clippings?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most lawns, yes. Mulched clippings break down within days and return nitrogen and other nutrients to the soil, which can reduce how much fertilizer you need. Mulching is also faster, since there is no bag to empty. Bag when the lawn is overgrown, when there is a fungal disease, or when weeds have gone to seed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do mulched clippings cause thatch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Thatch is made up mainly of roots, stems and runners, which decompose slowly. Grass clippings are mostly water and break down within days, so they do not contribute significantly to thatch. Excess nitrogen and overwatering, which encourage fast, shallow growth, are more common causes of thatch problems.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a mulching blade and a regular blade?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A regular, or high-lift, blade has a large upswept wing that creates strong airflow to throw clippings out of the chute or into the bag. A mulching blade has a longer curved cutting edge and extra cutting surfaces, with less lift, so clippings circulate under the deck and get cut several times into fine pieces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you mulch wet grass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but the results are poor. Wet clippings stick together and to the deck, reducing airflow and blade speed and leaving clumps. If you have to mow wet grass, raise the cutting height, walk slowly and consider bagging for that one mowing. Afterwards, clean the underside of the deck.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a mulching mower handle leaves?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you mulch them often. A thin layer of leaves where you can still see grass mulches easily into dime-sized pieces that feed the soil. A thick layer overwhelms the mower and leaves a mat. Mulch weekly through the autumn, go over areas twice if needed, and bag the excess for compost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are battery mowers good at mulching?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best ones are. Mulching needs high blade speed and enough torque to hold it as clippings build up. Premium battery mowers like the EGO LM2156SP and LM2206SP mulch about as well as good gas mowers. Cheaper models can struggle in thick grass. Expect runtime to fall well below the rating when mulching dense growth.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

